{"name":"Sundew","hex":"#e1cdae","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sundew","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of a group of insectivorous plants in the genus Drosera that catch insects by sticky droplets (\"dew\") at the end of hairs on the leaves and grow in boggy ground all over the world."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sundew"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sundew (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Sundew is the debut album by Mancunian band Paris Angels, released by Virgin Records in 1991. Following the group's arrival onto the British music scene during the popularity of Madchester, the album features the group's unique take on Madchester and baggy, combining Northern indie music with electronic instrumentation, techno and dance-pop. The album's cross-influenced sound is a result of each of the seven members of the band bringing their own influences. Much of the record, which was produced by Michael Johnson, was recorded near Wrexham, North Wales."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/22/ParisAngelsSundew.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sundew_(album)","wikidata":"Q65119048"},{"title":"Sundew (dragline)","blocks":[{"text":"Sundew was a large electrically powered dragline excavator used in mining operations in Rutland and Northamptonshire in the United Kingdom from 1957. It was the lead ship of a series of four Type W1400-series dragline excavators."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Sundew_dragline.jpg/330px-Sundew_dragline.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sundew_(dragline)","wikidata":"Q7639761"},{"title":"Drosera","blocks":[{"text":"Drosera, which is commonly known as the sundews, is one of the largest genera of carnivorous plants, with at least 194 species. These members of the family Droseraceae lure, capture, and digest insects using stalked mucilaginous glands covering their leaf surfaces. The insects are used to supplement the poor mineral nutrition of the soil in which the plants grow. Various species, which vary greatly in size and form, are native to every continent except Antarctica."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Drosera_spatulata_KansaiHabit.jpg/330px-Drosera_spatulata_KansaiHabit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drosera","wikidata":"Q266"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/DroseraZonariaTuber2-.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/DroseraZonariaTuber2-.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"A tuber of D. zonaria, a tuberous sundew, beginning its winter growth","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADroseraZonariaTuber2-.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Drosera_derbyensis_ne1.JPG/500px-Drosera_derbyensis_ne1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Drosera_derbyensis_ne1.JPG/1280px-Drosera_derbyensis_ne1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Drosera_derbyensis_ne1.JPG/500px-Drosera_derbyensis_ne1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"D. derbyensis, from the petiolaris complex","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADrosera_derbyensis_ne1.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Drosera_capensis_bend.JPG/500px-Drosera_capensis_bend.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://th